      {
        "thread_id": "TH2",
        "thread_goal": "Define the structure and core behaviours of the unified playbook.",
        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M19",
            "speaker_id": 4222,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Hi all, as per T2, I've put together a first draft outline for the Unified Customer Experience Playbook (A1). The link is in the project folder. Let me know what you think.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T2",
              "A1"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "eyes",
                "count": 3
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M20",
            "speaker_id": 394531,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "Thanks for getting this started. I've had a look. The structure is a good starting point. I'd suggest we organize it by customer journey stage rather than by channel. So, sections like 'First Contact', 'Needs Analysis', 'Closing', etc. Within each section, we can have specific guidelines for store, contact center, and field sales. This should help with consistency.",
            "mentions": [
              4222
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "bulb",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M21",
            "speaker_id": 200631,
            "reply_to": "M20",
            "text": "I really like that idea. It forces us to think about the customer's experience as one flow. We should also add a 'Guiding Principles' section at the start with our core behaviors and tone of voice. Simple things like 'Always confirm next steps' or 'Listen without interrupting'.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M22",
            "speaker_id": 45990,
            "reply_to": "M21",
            "text": "Yes, and it needs to be very practical. Lots of examples, maybe even short scripts for common situations. The content for T4 (call flow) and T5 (visit script) should fit right into this structure.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T4",
              "T5"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M23",
            "speaker_id": 49060,
            "reply_to": "M21",
            "text": "what is tone of voice?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M24",
            "speaker_id": 45990,
            "reply_to": "M23",
            "text": "It's about the 'feel' of how we talk to customers. For example, are we formal or friendly? Are we energetic or calm? We should aim for 'Helpful, clear, and patient'.",
            "mentions": [
              49060
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "thumbsup",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M25",
            "speaker_id": 4222,
            "reply_to": "M20",
            "text": "Organizing by journey stage is a good idea, I can see how that would work better. I'll restructure it.",
            "mentions": [
              394531
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M26",
            "speaker_id": 394531,
            "reply_to": "M25",
            "text": "Great. So, let's decide on the structure. How about this for the main sections: 1. Guiding Principles & Tone of Voice, 2. First Contact & Greeting, 3. Needs Discovery, 4. Presenting Solutions & Offers, 5. Handling Objections & Complaints, 6. Closing & Next Steps. Does this proposed format work for everyone?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D3"
            ],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M27",
            "speaker_id": 200631,
            "reply_to": "M26",
            "text": "That's a solid structure. Approved.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "white_check_mark",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M28",
            "speaker_id": 24573,
            "reply_to": "M26",
            "text": "Looks good to me.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M29",
            "speaker_id": 394531,
            "reply_to": "M27",
            "text": "Excellent, D3 is approved. Now for the core behaviors that apply to everyone. Based on the earlier chat, I propose: 1. Greet every customer warmly. 2. Listen to understand before responding. 3. Own the customer's issue until it's resolved or handed off. 4. Always confirm next steps. 5. Capture feedback accurately. Any thoughts?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D4"
            ],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M30",
